DevOps Issue: NGINX 502 Bad Gateway

“When NGINX throws a 502, it’s not the gateway’s fault — it’s what’s behind it.”

Today I troubleshooted a common production headache: 502 Bad Gateway in NGINX reverse proxy.

❌ Symptoms:

Frontend loads with 502 error

Backend app containers running but not reachable

Logs show connect() failed (111: Connection refused)


🔍 Root Causes Checked:

🔹 Backend app crashed or not listening on expected port

🔹 Mismatch between proxy_pass config and backend service port

🔹 Firewall/security group blocking internal traffic

🔹 Timeout too low for heavy requests


🛠️ Troubleshooting Steps:

1️⃣ Check NGINX error logs: /var/log/nginx/error.log  
2️⃣ Verify app port: netstat -tulnp | grep <port>  
3️⃣ Curl backend directly from NGINX host/container  
4️⃣ Adjust config: proxy_connect_timeout, proxy_read_timeout  
5️⃣ Reload NGINX after config fix: nginx -s reload

✅ Fix: Backend service was listening on 8080, while NGINX proxy_pass pointed to 80. Corrected config → service restored instantly.

💡 Lesson:
502 doesn’t always mean NGINX is broken. It means NGINX can’t reach your app. Logs + configs = lifesaver.
